# Mercure Hub - Development Environment
# Used with: local.Caddyfile (the fork's local-dev compose default)
# WARNING: Do NOT use with the production Caddyfile (admin conflict).

# === JWT Keys (HS256 default) ===
# WARNING: This is a well-known default - never use in production
# Algorithm defaults to HS256 when MERCURE_*_JWT_ALG is empty.
MERCURE_PUBLISHER_JWT_KEY="!ChangeThisMercureHubJWTSecretKey!"
MERCURE_SUBSCRIBER_JWT_KEY="!ChangeThisMercureHubJWTSecretKey!"
MERCURE_PUBLISHER_JWT_ALG=
MERCURE_SUBSCRIBER_JWT_ALG=
#
# === RS256 with PEM key (uncomment to switch) ===
# In compose.yaml: uncomment the `environment:` block — it overrides
# these HS256 values with the multi-line PEM via YAML | block scalar
# (Compose .env files don't support multi-line values, so the override
# has to live in compose.yaml's environment block, not here). All
# Caddyfiles already use {env.VAR} for JWT key/alg, so multi-line PEM
# values survive parse + Provision intact — no Caddyfile edit needed.
# For go:run (no Docker), set PEM inline (shell handles multiline):
#   MERCURE_PUBLISHER_JWT_KEY="$(cat fixtures/jwt/RS256.key.pub)"
#   MERCURE_PUBLISHER_JWT_ALG=RS256
#   MERCURE_SUBSCRIBER_JWT_KEY="$(cat fixtures/jwt/RS256.key.pub)"
#   MERCURE_SUBSCRIBER_JWT_ALG=RS256
#
# === RS256 with JWKS URL (alternative to PEM) ===
# JWKS URL takes precedence over publisher_jwt/subscriber_jwt if both are set.
# Add these to MERCURE_EXTRA_DIRECTIVES below:
#   publisher_jwks_url https://gist.githubusercontent.com/lzrf0cuz/e3002e3a7b561f546b5c331e52f729c0/raw/mercure-mock-jwks.json
#   subscriber_jwks_url https://gist.githubusercontent.com/lzrf0cuz/e3002e3a7b561f546b5c331e52f729c0/raw/mercure-mock-jwks.json
# Note: The hub fetches the JWKS at startup — the URL must be reachable.

# === Server ===
SERVER_NAME="localhost, host.docker.internal, mercure.compose.orb.local"

# Hub identifier — surfaces in per-hub health endpoint paths and metrics labels.
MERCURE_HUB_NAME=mercure-dev

# === Mercure Directives ===
# WARNING: DEVELOPMENT ONLY - DO NOT USE IN PRODUCTION
#
# CORS Origins:
# - Header auth (Authorization: Bearer): works with any origin listed
# - Cookie auth (credentials: include): REQUIRES explicit origin, * won't work
#   Add your dev server URL if using cookie auth (e.g., http://localhost:3000)
#
# JWT defaults to HS256 via publisher_jwt/subscriber_jwt in local.Caddyfile.
# To use JWKS instead, add publisher_jwks_url and subscriber_jwks_url
# directives to this block (see the JWKS section above for details).
# JWKS takes precedence over publisher_jwt if both are configured.
#
# === Transport Selection ===
# The "transport" directive inside MERCURE_EXTRA_DIRECTIVES controls which
# backend the hub uses. Swap by commenting/uncommenting ONE transport block.
# If no transport is specified, the hub defaults to bolt.
#
# Available transports:
#   bolt  — BoltDB, persistent, single-node (default)
#   local — in-memory, no persistence, fastest for dev
#   redis — Redis Streams, persistent, multi-node
MERCURE_EXTRA_DIRECTIVES="
cors_origins http://localhost:63342 https://localhost:63342 https://localhost
publish_origins *
demo
subscriptions
anonymous
transport bolt {
    path ./data/bolt.db
    size 10000
}
heartbeat 15s
write_timeout 300s"
# To switch to local transport, replace the transport block above with:
#   transport local
#
# To run with the redis transport, use `task dev:redis` — it loads the
# canonical redis-flavored MERCURE_EXTRA_DIRECTIVES from .env.dev.redis
# (single source of truth; do NOT duplicate the block here).

# === Caddy Global Options ===
# Dev tier: verbose logging + admin wide-bind. `admin :2019` is DEV
# CONVENIENCE — it makes admin reachable from the container's eth0
# interface so the compose `127.0.0.1:2019:2019` host-port forward
# actually delivers traffic, letting `curl http://localhost:2019/...`
# from the host hit pprof / config / health for debugging.
#
# Caveat (intentional dev tradeoff): wide-bind on a wildcard interface
# DISABLES Caddy's Host header check (`enforceOrigin = !addr.isWildcard
# Interface()`), so sibling containers on the compose network
# (prometheus, grafana) can reach admin endpoints without origin
# validation. In a single-user dev compose stack this is acceptable —
# the host port is bound to 127.0.0.1 only (no LAN exposure) and
# sibling containers are part of the same trusted stack.
#
# Production (.env.prod) leaves GLOBAL_OPTIONS empty, so Caddyfile's
# explicit `admin localhost:2019` applies — admin binds IPv4 loopback
# only and is unreachable from container eth0, sibling containers, or
# the host port forward. Host check is enforced via the Caddy origin
# allowlist for non-wildcard binds.
GLOBAL_OPTIONS="debug
servers {
    log_credentials
    trace
}
admin :2019"

# === Caddy Server Directives ===
CADDY_EXTRA_CONFIG=""
CADDY_SERVER_EXTRA_DIRECTIVES="tls internal"